00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.860
- There is one thing
that you will for sure do

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.070
very frequently in consulting

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.150
and this is conducting expert interviews.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.810
So on different types of projects,

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:55.020
it will be necessary for you
to tap into expertise of people

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.390
who have a lot of experience

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.980
in a specific industry or function,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.090
to get that knowledge

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.640
and consolidate that into insights

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.080
for your clients.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.880
So welcome to another video here

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.700
on my channel Firm Learning.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.000
My name is Heinrich and on
my channel I want to help you

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.020
to become successful in the
first years of your career.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.720
Today, we are going to do
this video in three parts.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.520
First, I'm going to talk about
what expert interviews are,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.940
what the contexts are
in which you need them.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.510
Second, I'm going to share a
couple of very insightful tips

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:27.990
on how to conduct them

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.420
and also how to find the
expert that you might need.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.490
And third,

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.950
I'm going to show you a tool

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.160
that you could do to do exactly that

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.850
because this video is
sponsored by Inex One.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.160
It's a platform where you can source

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.950
expert interview partners.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:46.010
So let's get into it. I trust
it's going to be interesting.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:46.843
Let's start

00:01:46.843 --> 00:01:49.290
by talking about why you
even need expert interviews

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.830
in professional services.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.160
Shouldn't a consulting team already know

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.540
all the things by themself?

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:56.540
Well, of course,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.830
it is usually the case that
on a consulting project team,

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.950
there will be consultants, project leads,

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.850
and, of course, especially,
also partners staffed on that

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.920
that will have deep industry
and function expertise

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.360
and will be able to
contribute significantly

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.010
to the project's success.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.480
Though what's also true is that you,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.750
in general, do not just want
to base your recommendations

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.100
based on the insights of
1 or 2 or 3 people or so,

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.160
but as a general best practice

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.170
to tap into knowledge of other
industry experts as well,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.710
ensuring that every person
who knows something valuable

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.760
can be staffed on a given project.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.930
But this is not only true for consulting.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:36.920
Another sector that also
very frequently relies

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.140
on expert interviews is private equity.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:44.100
So private equity is the
type of firms that purchase

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.580
other firms with the hope
to in a couple of years,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.130
sell them for an even higher price.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.290
And what these companies,

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.570
these private equity companies need to do,

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.830
is when they look for targets
to potentially acquire,

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.390
they need to have a very,
very solid understanding

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:02.730
of that company's industry,

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.000
of that company's market position.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:05.840
So what they do

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.290
is something that is called
a commercial due diligence.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.720
They look into the
company's business model,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.290
they look into how the company
is currently positioned,

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:15.820
how the market looks like.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:16.770
And here, again,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.770
surely they don't know all
of this for themselves,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.850
but very frequently,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.870
they tap into expert interviews.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.640
They reach out to industry experts,

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.250
people who really know the industry well,

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.290
to get their insights
and better understand

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.380
how the company is currently structured,

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.850
what their value proposition is,

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.140
and whether it might
be a worthwhile company

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.520
to acquire indeed.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.750
Now let's look into
some tips and insights,

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:41.670
what you can do

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.090
to conduct a successful expert interview.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.780
And the first question is:

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.520
What people should you actually
talk to for your project?

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.960
And, of course, it will
depend on the specific project

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.080
that you're working on.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.130
I will now focus

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.680
on due diligence type of projects,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.520
but this is applicable to
many other situations as well.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.530
So in these projects

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.740
I like to speak to three
different type of experts.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:08.740
The first is former C-suite
people or former sales directors

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.960
of direct competitors of the company

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.290
that you are actually interested in.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:14.520
If you talk to people
who worked at competitors

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.650
of that company,

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:18.430
they will likely understand
the market very well.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:21.980
Especially the C-level people
often have an overall view

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.390
on the whole market.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:26.410
And similar things are true
for the sales directors.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.680
Next will be former procurement directors

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.060
of companies that would be
customers of the company

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.320
that you would be interested in.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.150
And the reason is that
these procurement directors,

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.590
they will be the people that
will buy from the company

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:40.520
that you're looking into.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.690
So these people will very well understand

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.690
what the buying criteria are

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.100
and how the offering of this
specific company differentiates

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.810
itself from maybe offerings
of some of their competitors.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.160
When now you can understand

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.460
what the relative position
of strength of the company is

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:57.180
or maybe also some weaknesses

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.950
that the offering of this company has.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:04:59.800
Last but not least,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.960
you will want to talk to
C-level people of companies

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.707
in the supply chain of the company

00:05:05.707 --> 00:05:06.620
that you're interested in.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.540
So both the tier one and
the tier two suppliers

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.850
of that company,

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:14.920
just to get a better feeling
of how the company is perceived

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.230
by its biggest supplier partners.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.530
Another question is: How
should you approach them?

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.870
What questions should
you ask these people?

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.850
And here, two tips I'd like to share:

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.200
The first is, what's always key,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.600
is to have the end product in mind, right?

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.930
So with the results of that interview,

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.280
what do you wanna do with that?

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.980
What do you need to create based on that?

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:39.330
And in these types of projects
and these due diligences,

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.390
often it's a slide deck.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.280
It's a document that you create

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.730
that you will then later work with.

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.590
So what's always super
beneficial for this exercise,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.000
but also for many other similar tasks,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.520
is to already have an outline
of that document ready.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.670
Make sure that you have
prepared a straw man

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:57.010
of the document,

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:58.290
because then you exactly know

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:01.130
what the pages are that you want to fill.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:01.963
Ideally,

00:06:01.963 --> 00:06:03.730
you also what the structure of these pages

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:04.720
are supposed to be.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.290
Because then you can in a
very targeted way collect

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:10.660
the information that you need
to fill all of these pages.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.390
And if you want to learn
more about how to create

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:13.410
such a straw man,

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.110
I will link a video above here
that discuss this in detail.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:16.943
So check it out.

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:19.130
Now, my second tip is about the questions

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:22.260
that you are supposed to
ask in these interviews.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.190
And now I can talk a lot
about very specific questions

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:26.260
that you should ask.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.700
What I actually did as a service to you is

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.280
that I created a list

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:33.740
of the most relevant questions
for these expert interviews

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.890
that you can very well ask
these different types of people

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:38.030
that we just talked about.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.560
I compiled these questions in
a questionnaire cheat sheet,

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.790
which I would assume will be quite helpful

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.920
if you are planning to
conduct expert interviews

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:45.840
in the future.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.730
So you can find a link to
exactly this questionnaire

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.040
in the video description.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.780
So check out the link
in the video description

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:52.613
to download it.

00:06:52.613 --> 00:06:54.530
Now let's look into some behavioral tips,

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.150
what you can do to make the
most out of your interview.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.340
And now we'll skip all the
general interview tips and tricks

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:03.950
because I already created a
video about that in the past.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:05.580
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.880
I just wanna make two
very specific remarks.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.840
And the first is to be
empathetic with the interviewee,

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:12.430
given that for many of these people,

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:14.680
especially if they are external experts,

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.340
for many of them it might
likely be the first time

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.410
they conduct such an interview.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.750
So understand that for them
it's a rather new thing.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:23.360
Maybe they are nervous.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.320
Maybe they are a bit unsure
how to behave, what to say.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.850
So be empathetic with that

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:30.350
and make sure that in the beginning

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:33.250
you create a comfortable
conversation environment

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.660
where these people can slowly break up

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.440
and then talk to you and
share their knowledge.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:38.610
My second advice comes

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:41.070
from working with some junior consultants

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.250
who sometimes conducted
these interviews as well.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:44.600
And what I've often seen

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:47.500
is that some of these
people are like very strict

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:48.500
from the beginning,

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:52.220
asking super detailed
number-oriented questions

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.800
in the couple of very first sentences.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.700
This often is a bit difficult.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:58.440
It can often feel intimidating

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.050
and can feel like putting
the person in a corner.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.520
So you will lean from my
experience what works better

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.770
to create just this open environment,

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:09.210
is rather starting with
some more general, wide,

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.200
open-ended questions

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.010
where the person can
slowly get going sharing

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:15.260
some information with you.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.030
This will often be done easier.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:18.780
And also the likelihood is higher

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.980
that you really get the
information that you want

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:22.440
if you give the person some time

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.210
to ease into the conversation.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:26.190
Now, I hope you are convinced of the value

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:27.210
of expert interviews,

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.077
but now you might ask
yourself the question,

00:08:29.077 --> 00:08:31.870
"Where do I even get these experts from?

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.330
How do I find people that I can talk to

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:35.570
for these purchases?"

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:38.680
And usually in these
firms you have two sources

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:39.920
that you can tap into.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:41.570
The first is internal experts

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:43.730
and the second is external experts.

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:46.010
So let's start with the,
probably, easier one,

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.730
the internal experts.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.620
So, usually, in your network,
in the consulting firm,

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:52.960
you will find people who might be able

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:56.180
to contribute something
to that specific topic.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:58.800
You also might be able to
just write an email newsletter

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:00.890
to the interest group of people working

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.870
in that specific industry
or function practice,

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.160
to find another senior project lead,

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.480
another partner who has worked
in this specific industry

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:12.410
and has a lot of things to
share that you can reach out to.

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:16.430
But in many cases, the internal
experts will not be enough.

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:19.300
And indeed also, depending
on the firm you work for,

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.330
maybe your internal network
of people with expertise

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:25.530
in a specific topic is not even that vast.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:28.110
So here you need to reach
out to external people,

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:30.210
not working for your firm.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:31.860
So how do you do that?

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:36.130
And here, thank God, something
like expert networks exist.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:38.190
They are different service
providers out there

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.760
that you can reach out to,

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.490
and they will be able to
come up with a list of people

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:46.200
with a background in a specific industry.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:50.500
Often even people who have
worked in a very specific company

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:51.580
that you can target.

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:52.900
These are people that signed up

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:54.670
for a specific expert network.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:55.780
They'll let this network know

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:58.750
that they would be open to
conduct such an interview.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:01.660
Of course, it would cost
something. It's not for free.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:04.310
But then via these networks,
you can find these people.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.240
Of course, I hope from what I've said,

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.290
the issue with using these expert networks

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:09.570
already became clear.

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:11.530
And this is that it's
not really guaranteed

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.340
that only because you talk
to one specific network,

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.020
you really find the best profile
that you're interested in.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:19.210
And because there are
different service providers,

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:23.410
often you end up talking to
lots of different parties.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:25.800
They all have a slightly
different process.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.620
They send you lists of
potential candidates.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:31.990
It's a back and forth of many
different service providers.

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:33.260
I know this from experience,

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:35.950
having handled this in the
past, a lot as an associate.

